    { math: 'Hardy–Weinberg: the no-evolution baseline', note: 'Allele frequencies stay constant across generations IF: no mutation, no selection, random mating, huge population, no migration. Real populations break these — each break is an evolutionary force.' },
    { math: 'Natural selection: non-random survival', note: 'Alleles that help reproduction increase each generation. Sickle-cell allele persists in malarial regions because one copy protects — selection balancing costs and benefits.' },
    { math: 'Genetic drift: luck at small scales', note: 'Allele frequencies wobble randomly, hardest in small populations — founder effects (island species) and bottlenecks (cheetahs\' near-identical genes) fixed diversity by chance, not fitness.' },
