<TopicPage slug="subjects/biology/advanced/population-genetics">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Population genetics puts <strong>evolution into numbers</strong>: tracking allele frequencies
    over generations and identifying the forces that move them — selection, drift, mutation,
    migration. Its baseline (Hardy–Weinberg equilibrium, 1908) defines "no evolution", which
    makes evolution measurable: any deviation names its cause.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the four forces, anchored on the null model" widget="equationStep" config={popgenCfg} />

  <h2>What the field explains</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Why harmful alleles persist:</strong> heterozygote advantage (sickle-cell vs malaria), mutation–selection balance, or simple drift in small groups. Prevalence has a reason.</li>
    <li><strong>Conservation genetics:</strong> bottlenecked populations (cheetahs, Tasmanian deviors) lose variation and disease resistance; population genetics sets the minimum viable population sizes that guide reserves.</li>
    <li><strong>Human history in our genes:</strong> lactose tolerance swept Europe and Africa alongside dairying (~7,500 years) — one of the strongest recent selection signals in our genome.</li>
    <li><strong>Linking scales:</strong> population genetics connects Mendel's individuals (the inheritance lesson) to Darwin's species (the evolution lesson) — the mathematical spine of modern evolutionary biology (the "modern synthesis", 1930s–40s).</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Hardy (1908) & Weinberg (1908); Gillespie, Population Genetics: A Concise Guide; the Modern Synthesis literature.</p>
</TopicPage>
